Independent review Task004 S3: P2 Windows internal-source-path exclusion assertions at tests/codexHistoryProductPackage.test.js:353 and tests/codexHistoryProductFlow.test.js:362,415,922 compare raw backslash paths to JSON.stringify output, where backslashes are escaped. Read-only Node reproduction with p=require("node:path").win32.join("C:\\Temp","sessions","rollout.jsonl") and leaked=JSON.stringify({sourcePath:p}) produced existingAssertionWouldPass=(leaked.includes(p)===false):true and actuallyContainsPath=(JSON.parse(leaked).sourcePath===p):true. Frozen reports run in windows-pwsh and fixtures use path.join, so the assertion cannot detect an actual leaked Windows path. This proves a test oracle defect, not an observed production leak. Other source_ref-key checks do not fix this path assertion. Required correction: inspect decoded string values/object keys recursively or use consistent JSON escaping, and ensure a deliberately leaking Windows-path payload fails; retain actual event business paths. Counterevidence concerns C4/C4-package/T004-CK-C4-PACKAGE and C3/C3-read-final/T004-CK-C3-READ-FINAL. Historical execution PASS is retained; claimed non-disclosure coverage and prior clean review are contested.